~ stats.wit — the "why Wit is open at the bottom" section. Wit is a parser
~ that produces a typed AST; the renderers are just functions over it. This
~ section makes that point, and doubles as a live demo of the pattern the
~ user cares about: a table customised by CSS that is *defined locally* (as
~ the #local_styles component) and *referenced inside a wrapper div*.
~ Exposed as #page_stats.

~ Local styles for this section — defined here, referenced inside the
~ wrapper div below. Scoped by class, so they touch nothing else.
#local_styles
@@style
.spec-table { max-width: 780px; margin: 0 auto; }
.spec-table table {
  width: 100%;
  border-collapse: separate;
  border-spacing: 0;
  font-family: var(--sans);
  font-size: 0.98rem;
  border: 1px solid var(--border);
  border-radius: 12px;
  overflow: hidden;
}
.spec-table thead th {
  background: var(--accent);
  color: #fff8ef;
  text-align: left;
  padding: 0.85rem 1.2rem;
  font-size: 0.76rem;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 0.07em;
}
.spec-table td {
  padding: 0.8rem 1.2rem;
  border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
}
.spec-table tbody tr:nth-child(even) { background: var(--surface-2); }
.spec-table tbody tr:last-child td { font-weight: 600; }
.spec-table tbody tr:last-child td:first-child { color: var(--accent); }
.api-card {
  max-width: 780px;
  margin: 2.2rem auto 0;
  background: #1b1811;
  border: 1px solid #2e2820;
  border-radius: 13px;
  overflow: hidden;
  box-shadow: 0 18px 50px rgba(40, 24, 16, 0.22);
}
.api-card pre {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 1.3rem 1.5rem;
  font-family: var(--mono);
  font-size: 0.84rem;
  line-height: 1.7;
  overflow-x: auto;
  color: #d8cfbf;
}
.api-cap { max-width: 780px; margin: 1rem auto 0; text-align: center;
           color: var(--muted); font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.9rem; }
style@@
local_styles#

#page_stats
@section |class section| |id why|
  @div(class wrap)
    @h2 It's just a parser and an AST h2@
    @p(class lead) Wit parses your document into a small, typed AST. HTML, Markdown, and PDF are renderers over that tree — so when you need a different target, you write a function, not a fork. p@

    ~ wrapper div that references the local CSS, then the table it styles
    @div(class spec-table)
      @local_styles local_styles@
      @table |rows [[Format, Renderer, Output], [HTML, built in, clean semantic markup], [Markdown, built in, portable plain text], [PDF, headless Chrome, paginated pages], [Anything, a function you write, whatever you need]]| table@
    div@

    @div(class api-card)
      @@@pre
import { parse } from "@witlang/parser";

const ast  = parse(source);      // the whole document, typed
const html = renderHtml(ast);    // a renderer is just: AST in, string out
const mine = myRenderer(ast);    // so bring your own target
pre@@@
    div@

    @p(class api-cap) Every built-in renderer is written exactly this way. p@
  div@
section@
page_stats#
